WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:41.660
[Music]

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.833
[Narrator:] The nurse has a number of 
responsibilities in the prevention and control

00:00:45.833 --> 00:00:48.599
of communicable disease.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:54.366
In order to fulfill them she must have a basic 
knowledge of the natural history of disease.

00:00:54.366 --> 00:00:59.140
She must understand infection and contagion, and 
their relationship to man.

00:01:00.666 --> 00:01:04.400
She must know the various types of disease- 
producing agents.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:14.520
For instance, the animal and animal-like organisms 
such as the protozoa, the metazoa, and the arthropods.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:22.960
The nurse must know that plant-like agents 
including bacteria, fungi, molds, and yeasts

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:29.160
can cause disease, as well as those without 
distinctive plant or animal characteristics

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.120
such as the viruses and rickettsiae.

00:01:33.866 --> 00:01:37.320
While chemicals are not disease-producing 
agents,

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:43.340
they may cause injury to body tissues and 
increase susceptibility to infection.

00:01:44.466 --> 00:01:49.860
Not only must the nurse know that agents can 
cause disease, she must also be familiar

00:01:49.866 --> 00:01:54.866
with the methods by which disease is produced, 
whether by direct invasion of tissue,

00:01:54.866 --> 00:02:01.860
as in streptococcal infections, or through the 
liberation of toxin, as in diphtheria.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.133
She must recognize the various reservoirs of 
infection

00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:09.699
and the vehicles and vectors of transmission.

00:02:14.766 --> 00:02:20.099
For example, in a disease such as encephalitis, 
the nurse must know that the organism

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:27.266
is transmitted by a mosquito vector from its bird 
reservoir to a susceptible host,

00:02:27.266 --> 00:02:30.232
either man or animal.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:36.140
She must understand that a disease may be 
communicable during the incubation period,

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:41.500
the acute phase, or the convalescent stage.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:47.300
In some diseases a carrier state may develop.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:55.333
This state may be convalescent, as in polio; 
temporary, as in asymptomatic infections;

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:59.560
or permanent, as in typhoid fever.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:05.800
She must also be familiar with the portals of entry 
and exit of an infective agent.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.066
These include the skin and the mucus membranes 
of the respiratory,

00:03:09.066 --> 00:03:12.380
digestive, and genitourinary tracts.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:18.700
If the nurse is to know the natural history of 
diseases, she must be aware of the factors

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:21.980
affecting the disease process in the body.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:37.060
One, characteristics of the organism; two, host 
resistance; and three, environment.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:43.660
Significant characteristics of the organism include 
its virulence and resistance,

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:51.220
the number of organisms entering the body, and 
the rapidity with which it multiplies.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:57.800
Host resistance depends upon external body 
defenses, which include complicated anatomical

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:02.366
structures such as the skin, the eyes, and the 
ears.

00:04:03.466 --> 00:04:08.466
And the internal body defenses, the antibodies 
and other materials found

00:04:08.466 --> 00:04:12.699
in the body fluids and tissues.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:19.340
Other factors contributing to individual host 
resistance are age, sex, and racial heritage,

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:27.500
nutrition and general health, opportunity for 
exposure to a specific organism

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:33.580
through association, and the distance between the 
portals of entry of an organism

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:36.380
and the tissue for which it has specific affinity.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.860
Environment, the third factor affecting the disease 
process, includes the various influences

00:04:43.866 --> 00:04:46.466
in daily living.

00:04:46.466 --> 00:04:52.840
These may range from climate and occupation to 
social and religious customs.

00:04:58.166 --> 00:05:01.920
In understanding the natural history of disease, it 
is important that the nurse

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:06.720
also know the preventive and control factors 
affecting disease.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:14.733
These include measures directed against the infective organisms,

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:19.280
procedures to prevent the organism from gaining access to other hosts,

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:24.380
steps taken to reduce environmental hazards,

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:29.560
and methods of improving host resistance.

00:05:29.566 --> 00:05:35.466
Let's consider first the various measures directed 
against the infective organisms.

00:05:35.466 --> 00:05:41.660
One is mechanical removal of the organisms by 
either handwashing, filtration, or other means.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:48.000
Another is sanitization, the process of rendering an 
article aesthetically clean

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.066
and reducing the number of organisms.

00:05:52.066 --> 00:05:58.932
Disinfection--the inactivation or destruction of 
certain organisms by chemicals, heat, cold,

00:05:58.933 --> 00:06:03.533
or drying--is still another measure directed against 
the organism.

00:06:03.533 --> 00:06:11.799
Disinfection, however, does not always kill or 
inactivate spores, viruses, or all vegetative forms.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:19.260
The most satisfactory measure for destroying all 
organisms in or on an object is sterilization.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:24.233
Exposure to steam under pressure in an autoclave 
is a common method.

00:06:24.233 --> 00:06:31.466
Boiling, incineration, or heating, as in a dry oven, 
may also be used for this purpose,

00:06:31.466 --> 00:06:36.799
but the time required to attain sterility is often damaging to some materials.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:44.100
Even though these precautions are observed, 
some organisms do gain access to the body.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:49.320
In such instances the administration of medically prescribed drugs including antibiotics

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:53.360
has proved to be an effective preventive measure.

00:06:53.366 --> 00:06:58.466
A second preventive and control factor affecting disease is the employment of procedures

00:06:58.466 --> 00:07:02.066
to prevent organisms from gaining access to others.

00:07:02.066 --> 00:07:05.966
These procedures include isolation of the infected person

00:07:05.966 --> 00:07:08.999
and the practice of aseptic or barrier techniques,

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.766
which can prevent the transfer of organisms.

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:19.866
The third factor of disease prevention and control 
is the reduction of environmental hazards.

00:07:19.866 --> 00:07:25.999
The nurse contributes by supporting education, legislation, and corrective action.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:31.400
Finally, the fourth factor involves improving host 
resistance.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:38.000
While this is largely an individual responsibility, the nurse promotes it by active health supervision,

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:48.866
and by encouraging sufficient rest and relief from tension, optimum nutrition, personal cleanliness,

00:07:48.866 --> 00:07:54.066
limited exposure, and immunization against diseases

00:07:54.066 --> 00:07:59.860
for which there are available immunizing agents.

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:07.500
As a member of the health team, the nurse must apply her knowledge of the natural history of disease

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:13.066
when carrying out her activities in the prevention and control of communicable diseases.

00:08:13.066 --> 00:08:19.566
Her activities include adequate surveillance for early detection of disease,

00:08:19.566 --> 00:08:26.732
safe care of the sick to assure satisfactory recovery, enforcement of precautionary measures

00:08:26.733 --> 00:08:34.166
to prevent the spread of disease, and initiation of prompt epidemiologic investigation

00:08:34.166 --> 00:08:39.566
to find the source of infection and other persons who might be infected.

00:08:39.566 --> 00:08:45.366
She may also be expected to immunize a family 
and its contacts,

00:08:45.366 --> 00:08:50.000
give prophylactic and therapeutic treatment under medical direction,

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.800
and supervise the activities of disease carriers.

00:08:55.140 --> 00:09:01.100
At every opportunity she continues the process of 
education of the individual, the family

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:04.433
and community groups.

00:09:04.433 --> 00:09:10.333
She refers certain problems to other members of the health department, or to other agencies,

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:14.820
and reports her findings to the health officer and the attending physician.

00:09:16.566 --> 00:09:22.660
In addition to general measures employed in the 
prevention and control of communicable diseases,

00:09:22.666 --> 00:09:27.366
the nurse must employ specific procedures for 
each type of transmission.

00:09:27.366 --> 00:09:30.466
Where mode of transmission is unknown or uncertain,

00:09:30.466 --> 00:09:36.166
professional judgments must be relied upon to determine control.

00:09:36.166 --> 00:09:41.866
Where the mode of transmission is known, as that of the respiratory diseases,

00:09:41.866 --> 00:09:48.966
the nurse must try to minimize spread by contact, to reduce spread by fomites

00:09:48.966 --> 00:09:53.366
and to encourage immunization when practical.

00:09:53.366 --> 00:09:58.566
She must also try to reduce air contamination by 
proper ventilation,

00:09:58.566 --> 00:10:04.566
by safe disposal of nose and throat secretions,

00:10:04.566 --> 00:10:09.966
and by limiting activities which distribute organisms 
in the air.

00:10:09.966 --> 00:10:16.666
Skin invasions and infections can be diminished by avoiding close contact with infected persons

00:10:16.666 --> 00:10:19.732
or their personal articles,

00:10:19.733 --> 00:10:24.333
by combating or avoiding pest insects,

00:10:24.333 --> 00:10:29.799
by wearing protective clothing when exposed to insect-infested areas,

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:35.666
by avoiding careless handling of wild animals or their tissues,

00:10:35.666 --> 00:10:40.600
and by consistently sterilizing syringes, needles, 
and other equipment.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:49.400
Invasion of the genitourinary mucosa is best prevented by the practice of personal cleanliness

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:54.300
and the avoidance of promiscuous sexual contact.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:58.866
In those diseases which spread from the gastrointestinal tract,

00:10:58.866 --> 00:11:03.980
specific measures must be taken to break the chain of anal-oral transmission.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:09.980
These include preventing contamination of water, food, and milk,

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:15.440
sterilizing materials possibly contaminated by sick persons,

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:19.900
locating and supervising carriers,

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:23.880
and promoting personal cleanliness.

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:29.600
These, then, are some of the responsibilities of the nurse.

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:33.460
With her knowledge of the many factors affecting 
the disease process,

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:38.160
she is better able to assume these responsibilities

00:11:38.166 --> 00:11:43.532
and direct her activities toward the prevention and 
control of communicable disease.

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:52.180
[Music]

